  marking it as stub behavior that a real API response would replace. This is the one spot I'd call out: a
  client-fabricated ID is [3mnot[0m a trustworthy user identity. It's fine as a stub, and it's exactly the thing to delete
  when [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m gets wired up.

  [1mData flow.[0m submit → [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m (unchanged) → [38;5;153mlogin[39m assigns ID, calls [38;5;153mtrackLogin[39m, returns [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m →
  handler logs the result.

  [1mError handling.[0m Unchanged. Everything stays synchronous; no new failure modes. [38;5;153mtrackLogin[39m is fire-and-forget, so a
  future network-backed version must swallow its own errors rather than break a login.

  [1mTesting — the gap.[0m There is no test runner, no dependencies, nothing to hang a test on, and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m reaches for
  [38;5;153mdocument[39m at load time so it can't be required in isolation. I can't write a meaningful test without first adding test
  infrastructure, which is a larger change than you asked for. I'd rather tell you that than quietly skip it or bolt on
  a runner you didn't ask for.

